// The simulated driver: the machine's other half, with no machine.
//
// One cog stands in for the motor driver process. It is time-triggered on the
// bus cycle and publishes one pose sample per cycle whatever else happened,
// because that stream is the clock the control-rate cogs run on and a cycle
// with no sample is a cycle they never see. What it decides between those
// samples -- which goal is due, what to rewrite, when silence has gone on long
// enough to de-torque the machine -- is not written here: it is
// `reachy-driver`, the same decision the real driver process will run, hosted
// rather than re-implemented.
//
// What is written here is the plant: where the servos are, how fast they move,
// what a jammed one does, and what a cycle whose replies were lost looks like
// on the wire. It is a first-order model and nothing more -- no inertia, no
// voltage, no temperature -- which is enough to raise every fault the motion
// tick classifies from evidence and is not enough to answer a question about
// servo fidelity. When the real driver exists, logs captured from it supersede
// this model rather than argue with it.
//
// The scenario's hand reaches the plant through `SimCmdChan`, which is fed from
// an input log. Those injections stand in for the physical world and for the
// arming sequencer, neither of which exists in this system.
//
// The foot of this file is the composition: the box that closes the loop around
// all three cogs, and the process that hosts it. They are here rather than in
// the system module next door because the grammar admits a `system_target` only
// in a module that generates nothing, and a process needs a module that
// generates an executable.
//
// Every signal below is `multi_instance`, for the reason `motion.clk`'s header
// states in full: the marker is a fact about how this system is built, not
// about the machine, which carries one of each cog.
// TODO(clockwork-single-instance-signal)
// (A comment may not sit directly above a `use`, which is why this one is
// here.)

#![generate(cpp, cpp_cog, cpp_exe, cpp_test_cog)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::sim)]

use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] cogs::config;
use cogs::motion;
use cogs::msgs;
use @clockwork::clockwork::io::var_packet;
use @clockwork::std::signals::{ReportGroupPolicy, ReportingStrategy,
    ReportGroupLogType};

// What the driver did that the sample stream does not show: the edges. One
// publisher -- the driver -- as every channel in this repo's systems has.
//
// Sized sixteen deep against a view of one, which is more slack than the rate
// needs: events mark boundaries, not cycles, and a driver emitting one per
// cycle is a driver reporting a standing condition at poll rate.
channel DriverEvt
{
    message_type: Tachyon<var_packet::VarPacket64>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The scenario's injections, read from the input log. One publisher -- the log
// reader.
//
// Sixteen deep against a view of eight: injections are sparse, and the depth is
// what lets a scenario put a burst of them at one instant without the reader
// lapping the cog that reads them. What actually bounds a burst is the view,
// not the ring -- an injection the view does not reach is one the cog never
// sees, and unlike every other loss this cog suffers there is no count of it --
// so the view is sized for the largest burst a scenario has a hand for, an
// operation per joint group per instant and room over.
channel SimCmdChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<msgs::SimCmd>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The motor driver, with the motors modelled.
//
// Its cycle is the real one's: take in what arrived, decide what to write, write
// it, report what happened. One pose sample goes out every cycle without
// exception -- the heartbeat -- and an event goes out only when something
// happened that the sample stream cannot show.
//
// A cycle can raise more than one event and an output carries one message per
// execution, so the events are ranked: the dead-man's latch outranks the rest,
// because it is the machine changing state rather than a remark about a
// datagram, and the ones that lose the slot are counted. In the scenarios this
// runs in the two do not coincide -- a live goal stream is what holds the
// dead-man off.
//
// The counters below are the run's totals, held in the state slot and reported
// on the signal group only on the executions where one of them moves. Kept in
// the group's own fold instead they would restart every time the group
// reported.
//
// The `simulation_options` at the foot say how long an execution is modelled to
// take, which is the offset between a cycle's nominal instant and the log time
// of the sample it publishes. A checker reading timestamps has to know that the
// two are different clocks: `execute_at` arithmetic is off the nominal, and the
// log time is off the publish. It is stated rather than left to the generator's
// default, which happens to be the same millisecond: an assertion about a
// number needs somewhere to read the number from. (Neither a block header nor a
// field inside one may carry a comment of its own, which is why this is here.)
cog MotorSim
{
    configs
    {
        // The cycle, the dead-man, and the modelled servos' speed. The cycle
        // this cog is woken on is the execution condition's below; the period
        // here is what one cycle is worth to the plant, and a scenario that
        // sets the two apart is telling the plant that its cycle is longer or
        // shorter than the one it is woken on.
        params: Tappy<config::SimParams>;
    }

    states
    {
        // The gate and the plant, and the run's totals.
        sim: Tappy<msgs::SimState>
        {
            mutable: true;
        }
    }

    signals stats
    {
        // How many goals reached the modelled servos. A goal that arrived and
        // was queued is not one of these until its instant comes round.
        goals_executed: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many goals were refused because the queue was full. A sender
        // overrunning the gate, which the deterministic scenarios cannot do at
        // one goal per cycle and a stalled commander online can.
        goals_dropped: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times the dead-man latched torque off. The one counter here
        // about the machine rather than about the traffic.
        hold_timeouts: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many datagrams on the goal channel the codec refused. Distinct
        // from a dropped goal: those bytes were not a setpoint at all, so they
        // name no instant and no rows.
        undecodable_goals: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many events were raised on a cycle that had already reported one.
        // What a reader compares against the events in the log to know whether
        // it is reading all of them.
        events_dropped: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times a field of this cog's own slot named servos this build
        // does not know.
        refused_state_fields: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many of the scenario's injections this build could not carry out:
        // an operation it does not know, or one naming servos it does not know.
        // A scenario authored against a newer vocabulary than this binary would
        // otherwise read as a machine nobody ever touched.
        refused_injections: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }
    }

    inputs
    {
        // The goal stream, sized to see every datagram rather than the latest
        // one: a goal that went unseen is a goal the machine was never asked
        // for, and the gate is what decides which of them is due.
        goals: Tappy<var_packet::VarPacket128>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }

        // The scenario's injections. Sized to see all of a same-instant burst:
        // an injection outside this window is silently absent, where every
        // other injection this cog cannot carry out is counted.
        cmds: Tappy<msgs::SimCmd>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }

        // This cog's own sample channel, at the default view of 1: the last
        // sample it published, or an empty view before it ever has. The wire
        // sequence number of the next one is read back from it rather than kept
        // in a state field. Deliberately not a condition -- a cog woken by its
        // own publication would never stop.
        own_pose: Tappy<var_packet::VarPacket288>;

        // This cog's own event channel, read back for the same reason. Events
        // are sparse, so this view holds the last one however long ago it was
        // sent, which is exactly what a sequence number needs.
        own_evt: Tappy<var_packet::VarPacket64>;
    }

    outputs
    {
        pose: Tappy<var_packet::VarPacket288>;
        evt: Tappy<var_packet::VarPacket64>;
    }

    execution
    {
        // The bus cycle. Under the deterministic runner this is exact: the
        // runner advances simulated time to the timer rather than sampling a
        // clock, so the samples sit on a perfect grid and every instant a
        // checker asserts is arithmetic rather than measurement.
        condition tick: time_since_last_exec(20ms);

        execute when: tick;
    }

    simulation_options
    {
        execution_duration: 1ms;
    }
}

// The window a post-aggregated group folds over before it reports. It bounds
// observations, not time: these counters are written only when one of them
// moves, and every observation is an absolute total, so the first report of a
// fresh window carries the run's count and not the window's.
policy ReportGroupPolicy for MotorSim.stats
{
    reporting_strategy = ReportingStrategy::post_aggregated;
    log_type = ReportGroupLogType::event;
    max_observations = 1000;
}

// The control loop, closed: the simulated driver, the decision tick and the
// estimator, with the driver's sample stream as the clock all three run on.
//
// The topology is the one the real system will have, with two of the three
// publishers replaced. `motor_sim` stands in for the driver process, and the
// input log stands in for the session cog on `ScheduleChan` and for the
// physical world on `SimCmdChan`. Nothing the cogs do distinguishes those from
// the real thing: a cog reads channels, and a channel does not say what filled
// it.
//
// Every channel has exactly one publisher. The two self-loops -- the decision
// tick reading its own goal channel, the driver reading its own sample and
// event channels -- add subscribers and no publishers, which is what lets those
// cogs read back what they last sent instead of remembering it in a slot.
box SimBox
{
    new motor_sim: MotorSim;
    new mover: motion::Mover;
    new pose: motion::Pose;

    // Each cog's own slot, written by nothing else. No `init=`, so each is
    // default-constructed into a buffer the compiler synthesizes.
    new sim_state: State(representation=Tachyon<msgs::SimState>);
    connect sim_state to motor_sim.sim;

    new mover_state: State(representation=Tachyon<msgs::MoverState>);
    connect mover_state to mover.ctrl;

    new pose_state: State(representation=Tachyon<msgs::PoseState>);
    connect pose_state to pose.est;

    // The two configs, read from checked-in protobuf text before the first
    // execution. The paths resolve against the runfiles of whatever runs the
    // process, which is why the files are data dependencies of the executable.
    new sim_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::SimParams>,
        path="cogs/sim_params.textproto");
    connect sim_params to motor_sim.params;

    new mover_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::MoverParams>,
        path="cogs/mover_params.textproto");
    connect mover_params to mover.params;

    // The sample stream: the driver publishes it, both control-rate cogs run on
    // it, and the driver reads its own last one back for the wire sequence
    // number.
    connect motor_sim.pose to motion::DriverPose;
    connect motion::DriverPose to mover.sample;
    connect motion::DriverPose to pose.sample;
    connect motion::DriverPose to motor_sim.own_pose;

    // The goal stream, and the loop it closes: the tick commands, the driver's
    // gate decides what is due, and the tick reads back what it last sent.
    connect mover.goal to motion::DriverCmd;
    connect motion::DriverCmd to motor_sim.goals;
    connect motion::DriverCmd to mover.own_cmd;

    // The driver's edges, read back by their own publisher for the same reason.
    connect motor_sim.evt to DriverEvt;
    connect DriverEvt to motor_sim.own_evt;

    // What the run is asked to do, and what the world does to it. Both come
    // from the input log.
    connect motion::ScheduleChan to mover.sched;
    connect SimCmdChan to motor_sim.cmds;

    // Logged only: nothing in this system consumes either.
    connect mover.fault to motion::TickFaults;
    connect pose.estimate to motion::Estimates;
}

// The machine the whole loop runs on. One domain: this system is one process by
// construction, because the deterministic runner is a single-process runner and
// every channel in it is in-process memory.
cpu_domain MotionCpu;

// One box in one process: the topology the deterministic runner is documented
// for, and the one a `LogReaderPolicy` is permitted in.
box System
{
    new box: SimBox;

    // `sim_clk_exe` is the executable the compiler synthesizes for this file
    // under `cpp_exe`, named for the file stem.
    new proc: Process(executable=sim_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=proc) in box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=MotionCpu) to proc;
}
